A common scaling mistake independent of Context vs store: keeping API responses in their **nested** shape inside shared state.

```js
// Nested — the same author object is duplicated across every post
posts: [
  { id: 1, title: "A", author: { id: 7, name: "Ada" } },
  { id: 2, title: "B", author: { id: 7, name: "Ada" } },
]

// Normalized — one copy of each entity, referenced by id
posts: { byId: { 1: { id: 1, title: "A", authorId: 7 }, 2: { id: 2, title: "B", authorId: 7 } } },
authors: { byId: { 7: { id: 7, name: "Ada" } } }
```

Normalized shape (flat, keyed by id, referenced rather than embedded) fixes two problems at once: updating Ada's name means changing **one** record instead of finding and updating every post that embeds a copy of her, and it eliminates the class of bug where two embedded copies of the same entity silently drift apart because only one of them got updated. This is exactly the relational-database normalization idea, applied to client-side state for the same reason: a single source of truth per entity.
